{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>I am the Elohim of your father.</b> According to the plain sense, it is like \"the Elohim of your fathers,\" but it mentions the singular in place of the plural — the Elohim of every single father among your fathers, for all of them are called a man's father. And similarly <b>the Elohim of David your father</b> (II Kings 20:5), <b>This is my El and I will glorify Him, the Elohim of my father and I will exalt Him</b> (Exodus 15:2) — the Elohim of my fathers. And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 3:6) that <b>the Elohim of your father</b> is Abraham, for he was the first to call upon the name of YHWH, and afterward He mentioned him by name and joined the rest of the fathers to him. And the opinion of our Rabbis is: the Elohim of Amram your father (Exodus Rabbah 3:1), as though He said, I am your Elohim, and He wished to unify His name upon the righteous man who had died, not upon the living, and afterward, the Elohim of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob — for He is the Elohim of all Israel. And the reason He mentioned <b>Elohim</b> with each one, and did not say \"the Elohim of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob,\" is that He mentions His name and may His name be blessed and exalted. And I will further explain this in the portion (Ramban on Exodus 3:15)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:01:41.129757722Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}